Document (dev.stxt.website): Configuration files

	Metadata: 
		Author: ChatGPT 5.2
		Last modif: 2026-08-22

	Header: Configuration files

	Content >>
		Configuration files tend to start out simple and, over time, turn into a
		fragile mix of:

		* data,
		* implicit conventions,
		* comments,
		* and rules known only to whoever maintains them.

	Assert >>
		@STXT@ is designed for this point: readable, structured, and validatable
		configuration that does not turn into a programming language.

	Subheader: What kind of configurations

	Content >>
		Common cases:

		* Application configuration (server, ports, flags, limits).
		* Per-environment configuration (dev / staging / prod).
		* Pipelines and jobs.
		* Feature flags.
		* Service configuration (timeouts, retries, endpoints).
		* Documented configuration (long comments, context).

	Subheader: Common problems with other formats

	Content >>
		Known limitations of the most widespread formats:

		* JSON:
		  * Strict, with a syntax designed for machines.
		  * No comments.
		  * Hard to maintain as it grows.

		* YAML:
		  * Readable, but fragile with indentation.
		  * Several equivalent ways to express the same thing.
		  * Complex parsers and hard-to-predict behavior.

		* INI / properties:
		  * Flat, with no hierarchy.
		  * Implicit types and ad hoc rules.

		* XML:
		  * Verbose.
		  * Costly to edit by hand.

		@STXT@ starts from a different balance:

		* Minimal syntax.
		* Hierarchy by indentation.
		* Literal text blocks when needed.
		* Optional validation.

	Subheader: Example 1 — Server configuration

	Content >>
		Structured, readable, hand-editable configuration.

	Code >>
		Server Config (com.acme.server):
			Name: api-gateway
			Environment: production

			Network:
				Host: 0.0.0.0
				Port: 8080
				Public url: https://api.acme.com

			Threads:
				Min: 8
				Max: 64

			Timeouts:
				Read ms: 5000
				Write ms: 5000

			Logging:
				Level: INFO
				Format: json

			Features:
				Feature:
					Name: experimental-cache
					Enabled: false
				Feature:
					Name: audit-logging
					Enabled: true

			Description >>
				Main configuration of the API gateway.
				This file is versioned in Git and deployed along
				with the application.

				Critical changes must go through review.

	Subheader: What @STXT@ adds here

	Content >>
		* Node names carry the semantics.
		* The hierarchy is visible in the indentation.
		* Explanatory text lives alongside the configuration (`Description >>`).
		* There are no magic keys or implicit structures.

	Subheader: Example 2 — Per-environment configuration

	Content >>
		The same format for every environment, without duplicating the structure.

	Code >>
		Application Config (com.acme.app):
			App name: Billing

			Environments:
				Environment:
					Name: dev
					Database:
						Url: jdbc:postgresql://localhost/dev
						Max connections: 5
					Debug: true

				Environment:
					Name: staging
					Database:
						Url: jdbc:postgresql://staging/db
						Max connections: 10
					Debug: false

				Environment:
					Name: prod
					Database:
						Url: jdbc:postgresql://prod/db
						Max connections: 30
					Debug: false

	Subheader: Advantages over “copy and paste”

	Content >>
		* The structure is the same in every environment.
		* Differences between environments are located with `diff`.
		* It can be validated that every environment declares the same fields.

	Subheader: Example 3 — Feature flags

	Content >>
		A case where data and explanatory text coexist.

	Code >>
		Feature Flags (com.acme.features):
			Feature:
				Key: new-checkout
				Enabled: false
				Owner: Payments
				Description >>
					New checkout flow.
					Enable progressively per environment.

			Feature:
				Key: rate-limit-v2
				Enabled: true
				Owner: Platform
				Description >>
					New rate limiting system.
					Replaces the previous version based on filters.

	Subheader: Validation with @stxt.template

	Content >>
		A template (`@stxt.template`) covers three common needs in configuration:

		* Rejecting misspelled field names.
		* Checking types (numbers, booleans, dates).
		* Restricting the possible values (`ENUM`).

	Code >>
		Template (@stxt.template): com.acme.server
			Description >>
				Server Config: API server configuration
			Structure >>
				Server Config:
					Name: (1)
					Environment: (1) ENUM [dev, staging, production]

					Network: (1)
						Host: (1)
						Port: (1) INTEGER
						Public url: (?)

					Threads: (?)
						Min: (1) NATURAL
						Max: (1) NATURAL

					Timeouts: (?)
						Read ms: (?) NATURAL
						Write ms: (?) NATURAL

					Logging: (?)
						Level: (1) ENUM [DEBUG, INFO, WARN, ERROR]
						Format: (?) ENUM [text, json]

					Features: (?)
						Feature: (*)
							Name: (1) @Name
							Enabled: (1) BOOLEAN

					Description: (?) TEXT

	Content >>
		This template:

		* Rejects undeclared names (`Port` versus `Ports`).
		* Checks the basic types.
		* Leaves most fields optional.

	Subheader: Validation with @stxt.schema

	Content >>
		When configuration is a contract between systems:

		* agents,
		* external validators,
		* automated deployments,

		a schema (`@stxt.schema`) provides complete and explicit control.

		Usual workflow:

		* The template is written (more readable).
		* It is compiled to a schema.
		* The system validates against the schema.

	Subheader: Conceptual comparison

	Content >>
		@STXT@ for configuration sits between:

		* JSON → rigid and machine-oriented.
		* YAML → readable but ambiguous.
		* DSL languages → expressive, but able to execute code.

		@STXT@:

		* Executes nothing.
		* Evaluates no expressions.
		* Includes no external files.

		It only describes structure, data, and text.

	Subheader: Usage recommendations

	Content >>
		* Stable, descriptive node names.
		* Grouping by domain (`Network`, `Logging`, `Features`).
		* `ENUM` for environments, levels, and states.
		* Decisions documented in text blocks (`>>`).
		* Validation as an additional layer, not as an initial requirement.

	Subheader: Summary

	Content >>
		Configuration files are a natural use case for @STXT@:

		* People read and modify them without special tools.
		* Machines validate and process them unambiguously.
		* The same format covers everything from simple configuration to strict
		  contracts.
